===Etymology 1===
{{PIE word|en|h₂éd}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*tend-|id=stretch}}
From {{inh|en|enm|attenden}}, {{m|enm|atenden|t=to devote oneself (to a task, etc.); to pay attention to (something), to look after; to consider (something); to expect or look forward to (something); to intend to do (something); to help or serve (someone), attend upon; to take care of (something)}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=attenden|pos=v|id=MED2913}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|atendre|t=to await, wait for; to expect; to intend}}, from {{der|en|la|attendere}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|attendō}}, {{m|la|adtendō|t=to pay attention to, attend; to direct or turn toward}}, from {{m|la|ad-|t={{glossary|prefix}} meaning ‘to, towards’}} + {{m|la|tendō|t=to direct one’s course; to extend, stretch; to exert, strive}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=5114524740|date=December 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> {{doublet|en|attempt|tend}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Senses relating to caring for or waiting on someone, or accompanying or being present.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[care for]] (someone requiring [[attention#Noun|attention]]); specifically, of a [[doctor#Noun|doctor]], [[nurse#Noun|nurse]], etc.: to [[provide]] [[professional#Adjective|professional]] [[care#Noun|care]] to (someone).
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=X|stanza=41|page=146|passage=The fift [beadsman] had charge ſick perſons to '''attend''', / And comfort thoſe, in point of death vvhich lay; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Defoe Plague Year|page=98|passage=VVe had at this Time a great many frightful Stories told us of Nurſes and VVatchmen, vvho looked after the dying People, ''that is to ſay'', hir'd Nurſes, vvho '''attended''' infected People, uſing them barbarouſly, ſtarving them, ſmothering them, or by other vvicked Means, haſtening their End, ''that is to ſay'', murthering of them: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Pope Riches|page=14|passage=Is any ſick? the Man of Roſs relieves; / Preſcribes, '''attends''', the med'cine makes, and gives.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[wait on]] (someone or their [[instruction]]s) as an [[attendant#Noun|attendant]], [[servant]], etc.; also (''specifically of a [[gentleman-in-waiting]] or [[lady-in-waiting]] to a [[member#Noun|member]] of [[royalty]]''), to [[accompany]] (someone) [[in order to]] [[assist#Verb|assist]] or wait upon them; to [[escort#Verb|escort]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|bestand|serve|Thesaurus:serve}}
##: {{ux|en|Servants '''attend''' the king day and night.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|act=I|scene=iii|page=22|column=2|passage=I thinke your Lordſhip is not ignorant / Hovv his companion, youthfull ''Valentine'', / '''Attends''' the Emperour in his royall Court.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=III|scene=iv|page=267|column=2|passage=Madame, the young Gentleman of the Count ''Orſino''’s is return’d, I could hardly entreate him backe: he '''attends''' your Ladyſhips pleaſure.}}
##* {{RQ:Walton Compleat Angler|chapter=Epistle Dedicatory|page=13|passage=[I]f common Anglers ſhould '''attend''' you, and be eye-vvitneſſes of the ſucceſs, not of your fortune, but your skill, it vvould doubtleſs beget in them an emulation to be like you, and that emulation might beget an induſtrious diligence to be so: but I knovv it is not attainable by common capacities.}}
##* {{RQ:Johnson Rambler|issue=200|date=15 February 1752|page=119|passage=[He] told me, that he had promiſed to '''attend''' ſome ladies in the park, and, if I vvas going the ſame vvay, vvould take me in his chariot.}}
##* {{RQ:Prescott Philip 2|volume=I|chapter=Early Days of Philip|page=40|passage=In October, 1543, the Portuguese infanta quitted her father's place in Lisbon, and set out for Castile. She was '''attended''' by a numerous train of nobles, with the archbishop of Lisbon at their head.}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=V|chapter=XXIII|passage=[W]ith devoted loyalty, though with a sore heart and a gloomy brow, he prepared to '''attend''' William thither.}}
## {{senseid|en|to be present at}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To be [[present#Adjective|present]] at (an [[event#Noun|event]] or [[place#Noun|place]]) in order to [[take part]] in some [[action#Noun|action]] or [[proceeding#Noun|proceedings]]; also, to [[regularly]] [[go#Verb|go]] to (an event or place). {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Children must '''attend''' primary school.}}
##* {{RQ:Carlyle Sartor Resartus|chapter=Idyllic|page=68|passage=Andreas too '''attended''' Church; yet more like a parade-duty, for which he in the other world expected pay with arrears,— {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=I|chapter=II|page=177|passage=It was made a crime to '''attend''' a dissenting place of worship. A single justice of the peace might convict without a jury, and might, for the third offence, pass sentence of transportation beyond sea for seven years.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=V|page=62|passage=In the eyes of Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke the apotheosis of the Celebrity was complete. The people of Asquith were not only willing to '''attend''' the house-warming, but had been worked up to the pitch of eagerness. The Celebrity as a matter of course was master of ceremonies.}}
##* {{RQ:Mandela Long Walk to Freedom|page=15|passage=I '''attended''' a one-room school next door to the palace and studied English, Xhosa, history and geography.}}
## {{senseid|en|to turn one's consideration to, deal with}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To [[take#Verb|take]] action [[with respect to]] (someone, or something such as a [[concern#Noun|concern]], [[problem#Noun|problem]], or [[task#Noun|task]]); to [[deal with]], to [[handle#Verb|handle]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Secretaries '''attend''' to correspondence.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=15|passage=Edward Churchill still '''attended''' to his work in a hopeless mechanical manner like a sleep-walker who walks safely on a well-known round. But his Roman collar galled him, his cossack stifled him, his biretta was as uncomfortable as a merry-andrew's cap and bells.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} Of a (chiefly [[immaterial#Adjective|immaterial]]) [[thing]]: to be [[consequent#Adjective|consequent]] to or present with (someone or something); to accompany.
##: {{ux|en|a measure '''attended''' with ill effects}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Learning|book=2|folio=91|verso=1|passage=[I] haue alſo '''attended''' them vvith briefe obſeruations, ſuch as to my vnderſtandinge, offer noe violence to the ſence, though I knovve they may bee applyed to a more diuine vſe: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=53|passage=Piety ''VVhat moved you at firſt to betake yourſelf to a Pilgrims life?'' / ''Chr''[''istian'']. I vvas driven out of my Native Countrey, by a dreadful ſound that vvas in mine ears, to vvit, that unavoidable deſtruction did '''attend''' me, if I abode in that place vvhere I vvas.}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=1|lines=419–424|page=62|passage=Novv ſing vve ſtormy Stars, vvhen Autumn vveighs / The Year, and adds to Nights, and ſhortens Days; / And Suns declining ſhine vvith feeble Rays: / VVhat cares muſt then '''attend''' the toiling Svvain; / Or vvhen the lovv'ring Spring, vvith laviſh Rain, / Beats dovvn the ſlender Stem and bearded Grain: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Steele|issue=449|date=5 August 1712|page=211|passage={{...}} Fidelia answered with a frankness that always '''attends''' virtue: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Fielding Amelia|volume=III|chapter=Containing Many Surprising Adventures|page=141|passage=Our Food vvas attended vvith ſome Ale, vvhich our kind Hoſteſs ſaid ſhe intended not to have tap'd till ''Chriſtmas''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=I|chapter=I|page=55|passage=[W]ealth was an inferior object; but what glory would '''attend''' the discovery, if I could banish disease from the human frame, and render man invulnerable to any but a violent death!}}
##* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=Sing Cuccu Nu|page=47|passage=The most rapid and most seductive transition in all human nature is that which '''attends''' the palliation of a ravenous appetite. There is something humiliating about it. {{...}} Can those harmless but refined fellow-diners be the selfish cads whose gluttony and personal appearance so raised your contemptuous wrath on your arrival?}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|archaic|or|obsolete}} To [[look after]] (someone or something); to [[tend]].
##: {{ux|en|Valets '''attend''' to their employers’ wardrobes.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|act=I|scene=vi|page=375|column=2|passage=They [plate and jewels] are in a Trunke / '''Attended''' by my men: I vvill make bold / To ſend them to you, onely for this night: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=III|lines=536–539|page=130|passage=One ſeek the harbour vvhere the veſſels moor, / And bring thy friends, ''{{w|Telemachus}}''! aſhore, / (Leave only tvvo the gally to ''attend'') / Another to ''Laerceus'' muſt vve ſend, {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Followed by'' '''[[to#Particle|to]]''': to look after someone or something.
##* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton My Novel|volume=I|chapter=XVI|page=220|passage=Lenny still went to church—a church a long way off in another parish—but the sermons did not do him the same good as Parson Dale's had done; and the clergyman, who had his own flock to '''attend''' to, did not condescend, as Parson Dale would have done, to explain what seemed obscure, and enforce what was profitable, in private talk, with that stray lamb from another's fold.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[ready#Adjective|ready]] to wait upon someone or their instructions as an attendant, servant, etc.; also (''followed by'' '''[[on#Preposition|on]]''' ''or'' '''[[upon]]'''), to accompany someone in order to assist or wait upon them.
##* {{RQ:Plutarch North Lives|chapter=The Life of [[w:Sulla|Sylla]]|page=505|passage=[H]e entertained a gard of three thovvſand about him, beſides a band of young gentlemen of the order of knightes that '''attended''' alvvayes upon his perſone ready at commaundement, vvhom he called the garde againſt the Senate.}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=4|lines=539–544|page=138|passage=Th' officious Nymphs, '''attending''' in a Ring, / VVith VVaters dravvn from their perpetual Spring, / From earthly dregs his Body purify, / And rub his Temples, vvith fine Tovvels, dry: / Then load the Tables vvith a lib'ral Feaſt, / And honour vvith full Bovvls their friendly Gueſt.}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Marmion|canto=1|stanza=VIII|page=30|passage=[T]wenty yeomen, two and two, / In hosen black, and jerkins blue, / With falcons broider'd on each breast, / '''Attended''' on their lord's behest.}}
##* {{RQ:Southey Thalaba|volume=II|page=84|passage=Following on the veiled Bride / Fifty female slaves '''attend''' / In costly robes that gleam / With interwoven gold, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:MacDonald Sir Gibbie|volume=II|chapter=A Voice|page=84|passage=He {{...}} had sought his place in the live universe by '''attending''' on drunk people and helping them home through the midnight streets, {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Followed by'' '''[[at#Preposition|at]]''': to go to and be present at a place for some [[purpose#Noun|purpose]]; also {{lb|en|obsolete}}, ''followed by'' '''on''': to be present at and take part in an event.
##* {{RQ:Stanley History of Philosophy|volume=I|part=1|section=[[w:Chilon of Sparta|Chilon]]|chapter=III|chaptername=His Death, and Writings|page=72|passage=He died (according to ''Hermippas'') at ''Piſa'', embracing his ſon, victor in the Olympick games, of the cæſtus, the vveakneſſe of his age overcome vvith exceſſe of joy; all vvho vvere preſent at the great aſſembly '''attended''' on his funeralls, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Burke Works|volume=X|title=English History|subchapter=Chapter VII. Of the Laws and Institutions of the Saxons|page=347|passage=All persons, of whatever rank, '''attended''' at the County Courts, but they did not go there as judges, they went to sue for justice; to be informed of their duty, and to be bound to the performance of it. Thus all sorts of people '''attended''' at the Wittenagemotes, not to make laws, but to '''attend''' at the promulgation of the laws; as among so free a people every institution must have wanted much of its necessary authority, if not confirmed by the general approbation.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|author=Supreme Court of Canada|work=R. v. Côté|url=http://canlii.ca/t/fndv8|year=2011|accessdate=2016-05-08|passage=Around 12:15 a.m. patrolling officers Tremblay and Mathieu '''attended''' at the appellant’s home.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|author=Prince Edward Island Court of Appeal|work=R. v. Yeo|url=http://canlii.ca/t/gnr68|year=2016|accessdate=2016-05-08|passage=There were a few errors in the testimony of [a civilian witness] which the trial judge noted – one, that they '''attended''' at the Fairhurst residence the day before the robbery, and two, that Wakelin was with them.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Followed by'' '''to''': to take action with respect to someone or something; to deal with.
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=Going Abroad|page=108|passage=[T]he two captains, Peleg and Bildad, could '''attend''' to everything necessary to fit the vessel for the voyage.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Followed by'' '''on''' ''or'' '''upon''': of a (chiefly immaterial) thing: to be consequent on or present with.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida Q1|act=II|scene=ii|sig=D3|page=35|passage=But I aſſeſt the gods, your full conſent, / Gaue vvings to my propenſion, and cut off / All feares '''attending''' on ſo dire a proiect, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=III|page=29|passage=The country in the neighbourhood of this village resembled, to a greater degree, the scenery of Switzerland; but every thing is on a lower scale, and the green hills want the crown of distant white Alps, which always '''attend''' on the piny mountains of my native country.}}
# {{non-gloss definition|Senses relating to directing one's attention.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[listen#Verb|listen]] to (something or someone). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|behear}}
##* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|text=The diligent pilot in a dangerous tempest doth not '''attend''' the unskilful words of the passenger.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[give#Verb|give]] [[consideration]] to (someone or something); to [[heed#Verb|heed]], to [[pay attention]], to [[regard#Verb|regard]].
##: {{synonyms|en|mark|notice}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Followed by'' '''to''': to give consideration; to pay attention.
##* {{RQ:Swift Tale of a Tub|section=IX|sectionname=A Digression Concerning the Original, the Use and Improvement of Madness in a Commonwealth|pages=169–170|pageref=170|passage=The preſent Argument is the moſt abſtracted that ever I engaged in, it ſtrains my Faculties to their higheſt Stretch; and I deſire the Reader to '''attend''' with utmoſt perpenſity; For, I now proceed to unravel this knotty Point.}}
##* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=III|page=168|passage=I hesitated before I answered; when Frankenstein, who had at first been silent, and, indeed, appeared hardly to have force enough to '''attend''', now roused himself; his eyes sparkled, and his cheeks flushed with momentary vigour.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} ''Followed by'' '''to''' or '''[[unto#Preposition|unto]]''': to listen. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|notice|take heed|Thesaurus:listen|Thesaurus:pay attention}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1912|author=w:Rudyard Kipling|title=s:The Beginning of the Armadillos|passage='Now '''attend''' to me,' said Painted Jaguar, 'because this is very important. {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|obsolete}} {{non-gloss definition|Senses relating to waiting for something.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[look out]] or [[wait#Verb|wait]] for (someone, or something such as a [[decision#Noun|decision]] or event); to [[await#Verb|await]].
##: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:wait for}}
##* {{RQ:Dryden Indian Emperour|29|III|ii|passage=Three days I promiſ’d to '''attend''' my Doom.}}
##* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|text=the different state of perfect happiness or misery that '''attends''' all men after this}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[expect]] or [[look#Verb|look]] forward to (someone or something).
## {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} Of a thing: to be [[in store]] or [[lie in wait]] for (someone or something); to await.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[remain#Verb|remain]] and wait; to [[abide]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive|rare}} ''Followed by'' '''[[for#Preposition|for]]''': to expect or look forward.
## {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} ''Followed by'' '''for''': of a thing: to be in store or lie in wait.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[intend]] (something).

